{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2085404187/1/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16b78196\" lines=\"6-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid \"bar\" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).\n- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.\n- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.\n- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.\n- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.\n\nKey insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nRecolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.\n\nInput structure:\n- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.\n- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.\n- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.\n\nRule:\n- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.\n- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n\nKey insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation\nis inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"332f06d7\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nSlide a solid square through a winding channel to reach a same-sized target square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg (most common color). A river color forms a winding corridor.\n- Two same-sized NxN solid squares on the river: mover and destination, each a distinct color.\n\nRule:\n- The mover square travels along the river toward the destination.\n- It can only pass through positions where the full NxN area is river.\n- If reachable, the mover replaces the destination; otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable point.\n- The mover's original position is filled with the river color.\n\nKey insight: Chokepoints narrower than N pixels block the square, forcing it to stop short.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2ba387bc\" lines=\"6-21\">\n<description>\nSort scattered 4×4 squares into two columns by type: frames left, solids right.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Multiple 4×4 objects on bg.\n- Frames: colored 1px border, bg-filled 2×2 interior.\n- Solids: entirely filled with one non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Classify each 4×4 object as frame or solid by its interior.\n- Sort each group top-to-bottom by input row.\n- Stack frames in left column (cols 0–3), solids in right (cols 4–7).\n- Output is 8 wide; height = 4 × max(#frames, #solids), bg-padded.\n\nKey insight: Frame vs solid is determined solely by whether the 2×2 interior matches bg.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e453dd6\" lines=\"39-52\">\n<description>\nSlide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then shoot red rays from enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A vertical grey (5) separator column divides the grid into left and right halves.\n- Black shapes sit on the left side against a background color (bg).\n\nRule:\n- Translate each black connected component rightward until its right edge is flush with the separator.\n- After sliding, find enclosed bg regions (holes) within the black shapes that touch the column just left of the separator.\n- For each row spanned by such a hole, emit a red ray across the entire right half of the grid.\n\nKey insight: Only holes adjacent to the separator trigger rays — the sliding creates enclosures that act as \"nozzles\" projecting rightward.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nReorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n- \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n- Solid rectangles in the interior.\n\nRule:\n- Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n- If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n- Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n\nKey insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e376de54\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nAlign parallel colored line segments so they all share the same start and end positions as a reference line.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several parallel line segments (diagonal, horizontal, or vertical), each a different color, running in the same direction but starting/ending at different positions.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the dominant direction shared by all segments.\n- Pick the median segment (by perpendicular offset) as the reference.\n- Shift every segment along the shared direction so its start aligns with the reference's start, then trim or extend it to match the reference's length.\n- Grid size stays the same; isolated single pixels are untouched.\n\nKey insight: The reference is the median line, not the longest; all segments are snapped to its extent regardless of their original length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"981571dc\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nReconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.\n- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.\n\nRule:\n- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.\n- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.\n- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.\n\nKey insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"38007db0\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nExtract the unique cell from each lattice row into a single-column lattice.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into equal square cells by uniform gridlines of color L.\n- Cell interiors filled with bg; each cell has an inner sprite pattern.\n- Each row of cells has exactly one cell with a unique pattern; the rest are copies.\n\nRule:\n- For each lattice row, find the cell whose inner pattern appears exactly once (the \"odd one out\").\n- Construct a single-column lattice preserving the original row heights, containing only the unique cells.\n\nKey insight: The task is per-row \"odd one out\" — every cell in a row is identical except one oddball, which must be extracted.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1ae2feb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nExtend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.\n- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.\n- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.\n- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.\n- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its\n  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.\n\nKey insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9bbf930d\" lines=\"61-72\">\n<description>\nMove magenta particles along corridors formed by parallel colored bars.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg=7. Particles=6 in col 0. Colored bars on even rows form corridors on odd rows.\n\nRule:\n- Particle goes straight through bg cells.\n- When blocked by a colored cell, turns 90° into the perpendicular direction\n  that has a bg cell flanked by same-color bars. Stops at grid edge or full blockage.\n\nKey insight: Same-color parallel bars define the only valid corridors, so each particle follows a deterministic local trace.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2b83f449\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nSimulate tokens falling through a pipe network after gates rotate open.\n\nInput structure:\n- Wall (0), bg (pipe fill), gate (horizontal 1×3 bars), token (small edge pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Rotate each gate into a vertical blocker; gate cells become bg.\n- Tokens drop downward through bg, choosing the extreme column for their side (left→min, right→max).\n- Tokens settle at true pipe ends (adjacent to wall/boundary), packing inward.\n\nKey insight: Tokens select drop columns by entry side and land at wall-bounded endpoints, ignoring blocker-bounded ones.\n</description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
